            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Portuguese for the junction, then the Portuguese for to speed up."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: o travão",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "o travão — \"the brake\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "O travão do meu carro faz um barulho estranho. The brake on my car makes a strange noise."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "In Brazil: o freio. The verb is travar, to brake."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "travão is built on travar 'to hold back, to brake', which is usually traced to trave 'beam'; trave is inherited from Latin trabs 'beam' (EtymDB): a bar that holds something still."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
            }
          ]
        },
